<!-- Website teaching layer for android/compose/snapshot-system.md.
     The original chapter is rendered in full; everything here is added around it.
     Follows the chapter's model and the androidx.compose.runtime.snapshots API reference
     (takeMutableSnapshot, apply, withMutableSnapshot, snapshotFlow) checked 2026-10-25. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A cart screen keeps <code>count</code> and <code>total</code> in two state objects. A background update writes the new count, then the new total. A collector that ran between the two writes logs "3 items, $19.99" to analytics: a combination that never existed. The bug report says only that the numbers "disagree sometimes".</p>

Compose state is not a plain field. Every `mutableStateOf` keeps **versioned records**, and every read happens inside a **snapshot** that decides which version is visible, much like a database's multi-version concurrency control. That is how Compose knows what each composable read, and how several writes can be published together. This chapter opens up records, snapshots, observation and the bridges to coroutines.

<!-- @diagram d5dca69ea44655b5 -->
### Records, views and observation

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-stack"><div class="viz-grid" data-cols="2"><div class="node"><strong>Snapshot view</strong><small>global, mutable or nested: decides which record each read sees</small></div><div class="node is-key"><strong>Snapshot state objects</strong><small>a chain of versioned <code>StateRecord</code>s</small></div></div><div class="viz-grid" data-cols="2"><div class="node"><strong>Mutable snapshot writes</strong><small>go to records private to that snapshot</small></div><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong>Atomic apply</strong><small>publishes compatible writes together, or reports a conflict</small></div></div><div class="node"><strong>Read observation</strong><small>reads register dependencies for the Recomposer or <code>snapshotFlow</code></small></div></div><figcaption>A read selects a version; a write creates or reuses one; observation turns reads into invalidations.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after MutableSnapshot — Atomic Multi-State Updates :: animation -->
### Two writes, published as one

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">A mutable snapshot isolates writes until apply</span><div class="stage viz-cols" data-cols="2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Inside <code>withMutableSnapshot</code></span><div class="node" data-el="inCount" data-art="flow" data-art-role="storage"><strong><code>count</code></strong><small data-text="inCountV">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="inTotal" data-art="flow" data-art-role="storage"><strong><code>total</code></strong><small data-text="inTotalV">—</small></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Everyone else (global snapshot)</span><div class="node" data-el="outCount" data-art="flow" data-art-role="storage"><strong><code>count</code></strong><small data-text="outCountV">2</small></div><div class="node" data-el="outTotal" data-art="flow" data-art-role="storage"><strong><code>total</code></strong><small data-text="outTotalV">$13.33</small></div><div class="node is-key" data-el="reader" data-art="inspect" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>An observer reads the pair</strong><small data-text="readerV">2 items, $13.33</small></div></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{"label":"Before","caption":"count and total agree. Every reader sees one consistent pair.","add":["outCount","outTotal","reader"],"state":{"reader":"done"}},
{"label":"Enter a mutable snapshot","caption":"withMutableSnapshot opens a snapshot for the block. Reads inside it start from the same visible values.","add":["inCount","inTotal"],"state":{"inCount":"","inTotal":""},"text":{"inCountV":"2","inTotalV":"$13.33"}},
{"label":"Write count","caption":"The new count goes into a record that belongs to this snapshot. Outside, count is still 2: the reader still sees a consistent pair.","state":{"inCount":"active"},"text":{"inCountV":"3 (private)"}},
{"label":"Write total","caption":"The new total is private too. Without the snapshot, a reader here could have seen 3 items with the old total, the opening bug.","state":{"inTotal":"active"},"text":{"inTotalV":"$19.99 (private)"}},
{"label":"Apply","caption":"The block returns and the snapshot applies. Both records become visible at the same instant; observers are notified about the applied set.","state":{"inCount":"done","inTotal":"done","outCount":"active","outTotal":"active","reader":"done"},"text":{"outCountV":"3","outTotalV":"$19.99","readerV":"3 items, $19.99"}},
{"label":"If someone wrote first","caption":"Had another snapshot changed count after this one was taken, and the mutation policy could not merge the records, apply would fail (withMutableSnapshot throws) instead of publishing half the block.","state":{"outCount":"warn","outTotal":"warn"},"text":{"readerV":"never a mixed pair"}}
]</script><figcaption>For app state, one immutable <code>UiState</code> value usually gives the same guarantee more simply; snapshots matter when separate state objects must agree.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after snapshotFlow — Bridging to Coroutines :: visual -->
### Two ways to derive from snapshot state

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-cols" data-cols="2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title"><code>derivedStateOf { … }</code></span><div class="node"><strong>Is a <code>State&lt;T&gt;</code></strong><small>read it in composition</small></div><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong>Invalidates readers</strong><small>only when the computed value changes</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Owner</strong><small>usually <code>remember</code>, so its cache survives recomposition</small></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title"><code>snapshotFlow { … }</code></span><div class="node"><strong>Is a <code>Flow&lt;T&gt;</code></strong><small>collect it in a coroutine</small></div><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong>Emits</strong><small>when an observed state changes and the result is unequal to the last one</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Owner</strong><small>the collecting scope: <code>LaunchedEffect</code>, <code>viewModelScope</code></small></div></div></div><figcaption>Both re-read only the state their block actually touches; a captured plain local is invisible to them.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Common Pitfalls :: check -->
**Will it update?** Decide before opening each answer.

<details><summary><code>val name = state.value; LaunchedEffect(Unit) { snapshotFlow { name }.collect { log(it) } }</code></summary>

No. The block reads a captured `String`, not the state object, so no dependency is recorded. Read `state.value` inside the block.
</details>

<details><summary>A ViewModel field <code>var loading = false</code> changed from a coroutine; the composable reads <code>vm.loading</code>.</summary>

No. A plain field is not snapshot state, so the read registers nothing and the write invalidates nothing. Use `mutableStateOf` or expose a `StateFlow`.
</details>

<details><summary><code>val showTop by remember(listState) { derivedStateOf { listState.firstVisibleItemIndex > 0 } }</code> while scrolling.</summary>

Readers recompose only when the Boolean flips, not on every scroll position, which is exactly what `derivedStateOf` is for.
</details>
